---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Base
  title="ExCSV guide"
  description="What you write in an ExCSV file — types, grain, stats, SQL — and why it makes a table easier to trust."
  wide
>
  <article class="guide">
    <h1>Guide</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      What you can write in an ExCSV file, and why a data scientist would.
      Every line is optional. The rows stay CSV.
    </p>

    <nav class="jump" aria-label="Guide">
      <a href="#glance">A file</a>
      <a href="#header">Header</a>
      <a href="#columns">Columns</a>
      <a href="#grain">Grain</a>
      <a href="#aggregations">Aggregations</a>
      <a href="#sql">SQL</a>
      <a href="#open">Open it</a>
      <a href="#formats">Formats</a>
    </nav>

    <section class="block" id="glance">
      <p class="kicker">The idea</p>
      <h2 id="full-example">A file at a glance</h2>
      <p>
        You open a CSV. Which column is the amount? Is <code>01720</code> a ZIP code or 1720?
        Are these <em>all</em> the rows? What's the total — do you trust the preview, or re-sum it yourself?
      </p>
      <p>
        Those answers used to live in a README. Here they sit in the file, as <code>#</code> lines
        every CSV reader already skips. The data does not change. ExCSV only describes it.
      </p>

      <CodeBlock title="sales.excsv" description="Types, grain, a total, and the rows — one artifact.">
{`#!excsv version=0.4 delim=comma header=1
#@source: sales_db.orders
#@grain: one row per order
#column name=order_id type=int role=id
#column name=status type=string role=dimension enum=pending|completed|cancelled
#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD role=measure agg=sum
#column name=created_at type=datetime role=time
#%sum: ,,1050.50,
#$ddl-postgres: CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER, status TEXT, amount NUMERIC(10,2), created_at TIMESTAMPTZ)
order_id,status,amount,created_at
1,completed,500.00,2026-01-15T09:30:00Z
2,completed,300.50,2026-02-20T14:00:00Z
3,pending,250.00,2026-03-01T11:45:00Z`}
      </CodeBlock>

      <p>
        pandas: <code>pd.read_csv("sales.excsv", comment="#")</code>.
        Excel and <code>awk</code> see ordinary CSV. An ExCSV-aware tool also gets the schema and the sum.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="header">
      <h2>The first line</h2>
      <p>
        <code>#!excsv</code> names the dialect so nobody has to sniff it.
        Leave out what you don't need — defaults fill in (comma, UTF-8, first row is a header).
      </p>
      <pre><code><span class="hl-meta">#!excsv version=0.4 delim=comma header=1 rows=18432 null=NA</span></code></pre>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>You set this…</th><th>…when</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>delim</code></td><td>It isn't a comma. Names: <code>comma</code>, <code>tab</code>, <code>pipe</code>, <code>semicolon</code> — or the character itself (<code>delim=::</code>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>quote</code></td><td>Fields are quoted. <code>double</code>, <code>single</code>, or <code>none</code> (default).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>header=0</code></td><td>There is no header row. Identify columns by <code>index=</code> instead of <code>name=</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>null=NA</code></td><td>Your dump uses a sentinel. Empty cells are already null.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>rows</code></td><td>You want the count in the header — useful when a viewer only loaded the first thousand.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sql-dialect</code></td><td>Untagged <code>#$</code> lines should default to <code>postgres</code>, <code>duckdb</code>, …</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>reference</code></td><td>This file is a <a href="/variants/#sidecar">sidecar</a> — it points at the CSV, it doesn't contain rows.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Tabs usually live in <code>.extsv</code>. Invented keys go as <code>x-team=analytics</code>.
        An optional <code>checksum=sha256:…</code> fingerprints the data section — a heads-up if the rows moved, not a lock.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="columns">
      <h2>Columns — stop guessing</h2>
      <p>
        One <code>#column</code> line per column you care about. Annotate three columns and skip the rest — that's fine.
        No <code>#column</code> lines at all is still a valid file. It's just CSV with a header.
      </p>
      <pre><code><span class="hl-meta">#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD role=measure agg=sum</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=zip type=string</span></code></pre>
      <p>
        That's the answer to "is <code>01720</code> a number" and "is that float money."
        <code>decimal</code> is exact; <code>float</code> / <code>double</code> are binary floats.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Type</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>string</code></td><td>Text, ZIP codes, IDs that look numeric</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>int</code> / <code>long</code></td><td>Counts, integer keys (32- / 64-bit)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>decimal</code></td><td>Money, anything that must not round</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>float</code> / <code>double</code></td><td>Measurements where binary float is fine</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td><code>true</code>/<code>false</code> (also <code>1</code>/<code>0</code>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>date</code> / <code>time</code> / <code>datetime</code></td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>, <code>HH:MM:SS</code>, ISO 8601</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>uuid</code> / <code>binary</code></td><td>UUIDs; base64 bytes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Role — a number you sum vs a number you never sum</h3>
      <p>
        <code>type</code> is storage. <code>role</code> is analysis. An <code>int</code> can be an id or a count;
        only the role tells you which.
      </p>
      <pre><code><span class="hl-meta">#column name=order_id   type=int      role=id</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=status     type=string   role=dimension enum=pending|completed|cancelled</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=amount     type=decimal  role=measure agg=sum</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=balance    type=decimal  role=measure agg=avg</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=created_at type=datetime role=time</span></code></pre>

      <div class="cards cards-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3><code>id</code></h3>
          <p>Identifies a row. Count, distinct, join. Never sum.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3><code>dimension</code></h3>
          <p>A category. Group by, filter, count distinct. <code>enum=</code> lists the closed set.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3><code>measure</code></h3>
          <p>A number to aggregate. <code>agg=</code> says how: <code>sum</code> (revenue), <code>avg</code> (balance, price — don't add months), <code>min</code>/<code>max</code>, <code>none</code> (a ratio).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3><code>time</code></h3>
          <p>The axis. Group by period, trend, range.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Also useful when you have them: <code>title</code> (display name), <code>unique</code>,
        <code>required</code>, <code>min</code>/<code>max</code>, <code>format=0.00</code>,
        <code>separator=|</code> if a cell packs several values.
        <code>default=</code> is what a database would fill in on insert — it is <em>not</em> applied when you read the file.
        An empty cell is null. The file describes reality; it doesn't patch it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="grain">
      <h2>Grain — what one row is</h2>
      <p>
        <code>#@grain</code> is the most useful line you'll write.
        <code>one row per order</code> vs <code>one row per customer per day</code> is the difference
        between a correct join and a silent double-count.
      </p>
      <pre><code><span class="hl-meta">#@grain: one row per order</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#@source: sales_db.orders</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#@exported: 2026-03-24T14:30:00Z</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#@tags: sales,Q1,2026</span></code></pre>
      <p>
        Other <code>#@</code> lines are provenance: <code>source</code>, <code>author</code>, <code>created</code>,
        <code>exported</code>, <code>license</code>, <code>tool</code>, <code>comment</code>, <code>tags</code>.
        Invent a key if you need one — it's kept as-is.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="aggregations">
      <h2>Aggregations — the total is a fact</h2>
      <p>
        A <code>#%</code> line is a pre-computed statistic for the <em>whole</em> dataset,
        laid out like a CSV row so it lines up under the columns:
      </p>
      <pre><code><span class="hl-meta">#%count_nonnull: ,98,100</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#%sum: ,,154280.50</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#%avg: ,,1542.81</span></code></pre>
      <p>
        Column 3 sums to 154,280.50. Empty slots mean "not this column."
        Nulls sit out, same as SQL.
      </p>
      <p>
        Paste the first 50 rows of a million-row table into a notebook or a chat.
        <code>#%sum</code> still tells the truth about the full file.
        The visible slice can't lie to you. That's the difference between
        "the sum of what I can see" and "the sum of the data."
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>On</th><th>You can write</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Anything</td><td><code>count_nonnull</code>, <code>count_null</code>, <code>count_distinct</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Numbers</td><td><code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Strings</td><td><code>len_min</code>, <code>len_max</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="sql">
      <h2>SQL — the recipe travels too</h2>
      <p>
        Recreating a table from a CSV usually means retyping the schema.
        Here the <code>CREATE TABLE</code> (and the <code>SELECT</code> that produced the dump) sit in the file.
      </p>
      <pre><code><span class="hl-meta">#!excsv version=0.4 sql-dialect=postgres</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#$ddl: CREATE TABLE orders (id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY, amount NUMERIC(8,2))</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#$dql: SELECT id, amount FROM sales.orders WHERE exported_at &gt; '2026-01-01'</span></code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>ddl</code> is schema — run it if you want the table back.
        <code>dql</code> is provenance: how the extract was pulled, not something that runs itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Several databases, side by side: <code>#$ddl-mysql:</code>, <code>#$ddl-clickhouse:</code>, <code>#$ddl-duckdb:</code>.
        A consumer picks theirs. Tools only print the SQL — they don't touch your database.
      </p>
      <pre><code>excsv sql ddl postgres data.excsv | psql</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="open">
      <h2>Open it</h2>
      <p>The data section is ordinary CSV. Everything self-describing is a <code>#</code> line above it.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th></th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>pandas</td><td><code>pd.read_csv("sales.excsv", comment="#")</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>polars</td><td><code>pl.read_csv("sales.excsv", comment_prefix="#")</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Excel / Tableau</td><td>Open the data section, or keep a <a href="/variants/#sidecar">sidecar</a> and open the <code>.csv</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>awk</code> / <code>grep</code></td><td>Meta lines start with <code>#</code> — they skip them for free</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ZIP</td><td><code>pd.read_csv("sales.excsv.zip", comment="#")</code> — schema also sits in the ZIP comment</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>More patterns on <a href="/examples/">Examples</a>. A CLI that understands the metadata: <a href="/tools/">tools</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="formats">
      <h2>How it ships</h2>
      <p>Same language. Five formats — details on <a href="/variants/">Formats</a>.</p>
      <div class="cards cards-5">
        <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#plain">
          <span class="ext">.excsv</span>
          <h3>Plain</h3>
          <p>Schema and rows in one file. The default.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#sidecar" id="sidecar">
          <span class="ext">.excsv + .csv</span>
          <h3>Sidecar</h3>
          <p>Annotate a CSV you can't rewrite.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#zip" id="zip-container">
          <span class="ext">.excsv.zip</span>
          <h3>ZIP</h3>
          <p>Compressed. Peek schema without unzipping.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#pack" id="pack-container">
          <span class="ext">.excsv.pack.zip</span>
          <h3>Pack</h3>
          <p>Columns and several tables in one archive.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="card card-link" href="/variants/#json" id="json-form">
          <span class="ext">.excsv.json</span>
          <h3>JSON</h3>
          <p>The same document as JSON. APIs, config, AI output.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="json-profile">
      <h2>The JSON form</h2>
      <p>
        Same content — columns, types, aggregates, SQL — in a file of its own,
        <code>sales.excsv.json</code>, for when you're already in JSON: an API, a config, a prompt.
        There's a <a href="/schema/excsv-0.4.schema.json">JSON Schema</a> to validate against,
        or to constrain a model's output to. CSV → JSON → CSV doesn't lose anything.
        Money stays a string so it doesn't become a float.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`{
  "excsv": "0.4",
  "meta": { "grain": "one row per order" },
  "columns": [
    { "index": 0, "name": "amount", "type": "decimal", "unit": "USD", "role": "measure", "agg": "sum" }
  ],
  "aggregates": { "sum": ["1050.50"] },
  "data": [[1, "500.00"], [2, "550.50"]]
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>More on <a href="/variants/#json">Formats</a> · <a href="/convert/">Converter</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="block inspired" id="prior-art">
      <h2>Inspired by</h2>
      <div class="cards cards-3">
        <div>
          <h3><a href="https://docs.astropy.org/en/stable/io/ascii/ecsv.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ECSV</a></h3>
          <p>Astropy showed a CSV can carry schema as <code>#</code> lines. ExCSV keeps the shape, drops the YAML.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3><a href="https://docs.influxdata.com/influxdb/v2/reference/syntax/annotated-csv/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Annotated CSV</a></h3>
          <p>InfluxDB's one-value-per-column annotation rows. That's where <code>#%</code> comes from.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3><a href="https://github.com/MetaCSV/MetaCSV" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MetaCSV</a></h3>
          <p>A sibling file beside plain CSV. Same job as <a href="/variants/#sidecar">sidecar</a>, different vocabulary.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <p class="more">
      Worked files on <a href="/examples/">Examples</a>.
      Parser rules live in the
      <a href="https://github.com/boligolov/excsv/tree/main/docs/implementation">implementation docs</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .guide h1 {
    margin-top: 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .jump {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  .jump a {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.35em 0.75em;
    border-radius: 6px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .jump a:hover {
    color: var(--color-text);
    border-color: var(--color-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .block {
    padding-top: 0.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 2.25rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 4.5rem;
  }
  .block h2 {
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
  }
  .kicker {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .cards-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  .cards-3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  .cards-4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
  .cards-5 { grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr); }
  .card {
    background: var(--color-card-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 1.15rem 1.2rem 1.25rem;
  }
  .card h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
  }
  .card p {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .card-link {
    display: block;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .card-link:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .ext {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
  }

  .inspired {
    padding-top: 1.75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .inspired .cards {
    gap: 1.5rem 1.75rem;
  }
  .inspired .cards > div {
    background: none;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .inspired h3 a {
    color: var(--color-text);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .inspired h3 a:hover { color: var(--color-accent); }

  .more {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    padding-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 1100px) {
    .cards-5 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .cards-4,
    .cards-5 { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
    .cards-3 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .cards-4,
    .cards-5,
    .cards-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
